      "writtenOn": "2021-01-08T15:47:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The current code allows the user to delete *all* the keys, yes. But it does not allow the user to delete some of the keys without restarting the iteration. I think that would be useful. So if you think my suggested code will work, we could allow deletion and add this in the docs:\n\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\nThe given BiConsumer may to delete the passed-in entry, but is not allowed to perform any other structural modifications to the map, such as adding entries or deleting other entries. Otherwise, iteration will result in undefined behaviour.\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n\nThe reason I suggest this is that it\u0027s consistent with the general behaviour of Java iterators, which allow deletion, as long as you *only* delete through the iterator, and don\u0027t change the map in any other way. For example, see:\n\nhttps://docs.oracle.com/javase/7/docs/api/java/util/ListIterator.html#remove()\n\nAlso see the docs for ArrayList:\n\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\nThe iterators returned by this class\u0027s iterator and listIterator methods are fail-fast: if the list is structurally modified at any time after the iterator is created, in any way except through the iterator\u0027s own remove or add methods, the iterator will throw a ConcurrentModificationException. Thus, in the face of concurrent modification, the iterator fails quickly and cleanly, rather than risking arbitrary, non-deterministic behavior at an undetermined time in the future.\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n\nNote the \"in any way except through the iterator\u0027s own remove or add methods\". That implies that it is allowed to delete.",
      "parentUuid": "5ad56dda_cb327126",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 180,
        "startChar": 98,
        "endLine": 180,
        "endChar": 99
      },
      "revId": "b130a862a5ded162a3a3d1945098eed04b6044af",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
